---
export const prerender = true;
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../components/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';

const slug = 'tesseract-js-browser-ocr-image-quality';
const title = 'How I Improve Tesseract.js OCR Results in a Browser';
const description =
  'Brendan Chambers tests blur, contrast, cropping, tiny text, glare, and language choice to show what improved Tesseract.js OCR results in a browser.';
const imagePath = '/social/tesseract-js-browser-ocr-image-quality.webp';
const socialImagePath = '/social/tesseract-js-browser-ocr-image-quality.png';
const imageAlt =
  'Full-body smoke-kawaii girl comparing six browser OCR image-quality tests beside a laptop, magnifying glass, glare panel, crop marks, and privacy shield.';
const sources = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js', label: 'Tesseract.js official repository' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/api.md', label: 'Tesseract.js worker API' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/local-installation.md', label: 'Tesseract.js local installation guide' },
  { href: 'https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html', label: 'Tesseract OCR image-quality guide' },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  {
    href: '/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/',
    label: 'Image to Text OCR Tool',
    description: 'Run browser-side OCR with the same six language choices used in my test harness.',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/how-to-use-image-to-text-ocr-tool/',
    label: 'Image to Text OCR guide',
    description: 'Follow the practical tool steps and learn which results need a manual check.',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy/',
    label: 'Browser AI vs local AI',
    description: 'See what browser-side processing does and does not prove about privacy.',
  },
  {
    href: '/categories/ai-tools/',
    label: 'Browser AI tools',
    description: 'Explore AI helpers with clear model, privacy, and accuracy limits.',
  },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  slug={slug}
  title={title}
  seoTitle="Improve Tesseract.js OCR Results in a Browser: 6 Tests"
  description={description}
  breadcrumbLabel="Improve Tesseract.js browser OCR"
  imagePath={imagePath}
  socialImagePath={socialImagePath}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  imageCaption="The smoke-kawaii mascot compares the six image conditions I tested: blur, low contrast, cropping, tiny text, glare, and language choice."
  sources={sources}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      I built six fake receipts and document snippets to find out which image problems made browser
      OCR stumble. Glare caused ten character edits. Cropping lost two letters. The matching Spanish
      model restored two accents that the English run missed.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Quick answer</h2>
    <p>
      Give OCR complete characters, even lighting, enough resolution, and the right language model.
      Crop away empty surroundings, but do not clip the first or last letter. Check totals, names,
      dates, decimal points, and accented words against the original image before using the text.
    </p>
    <p>
      My fixed six-image test did not show every expected failure. The blurred, low-contrast, and
      tiny-text samples were still readable. That result matters because image advice should come
      from the exact file in front of you, not from a rule that promises every edit will help.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>I made six controlled images instead of using private documents</h2>
    <p>
      I generated six synthetic PNG files with known text. Each image changed one condition: blur,
      contrast, crop, text size, glare, or language. The English cases used invoice, order, product,
      and receipt lines with fixed numbers. The language case used Spanish words with two accents.
    </p>
    <p>
      I ran Tesseract.js 7.0.0 with the same self-hosted English and Spanish language files used by
      Access Free Tools. I compared the recognized text with the expected text after normalizing
      letter case and repeated spaces. Accents still counted as real characters. The edit count
      therefore shows how many character insertions, removals, or substitutions a person would need.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Tesseract.js runs recognition through a worker. Its API also notes that a higher-resolution
      image can improve results. See the <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/api.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">official worker API</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>The six OCR results</h2>
    <div class="editorial-table-shell" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Tesseract.js OCR experiment results">
      <table class="editorial-comparison-table ocr-results-table">
        <caption>Character edits and Tesseract confidence from my fixed synthetic test</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Image condition</th>
            <th scope="col">Manual edits</th>
            <th scope="col">Confidence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Blurred screenshot</th>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>96%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Low contrast</th>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>95%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Cropped first characters</th>
            <td>2</td>
            <td>92%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Tiny text</th>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>94%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Glare across the total</th>
            <td>10</td>
            <td>88%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Spanish with English model</th>
            <td>2</td>
            <td>93%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Spanish with Spanish model</th>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>96%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Confidence is Tesseract's own estimate. It did not replace the text comparison. An 88% result
      still preserved the receipt number and amount, yet it dropped part of the surrounding line.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Glare did the most damage</h2>
    <p>
      My glare image expected <code>RECEIPT 6201 TOTAL $57.80 CARD APPROVED</code>. Tesseract returned{' '}
      <code>RECEIPT 6201 TOTA $57.80 CARD</code>. The amount survived, but one letter and the final
      word disappeared. The result needed ten character edits.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sharper camera does not fix light that covers the characters. I would move the document away
      from a lamp or window, tilt the phone, and take another photo. If that is impossible, I would
      inspect every word that crosses the bright patch instead of trusting the confidence score.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Cropping helps only while the characters stay inside</h2>
    <p>
      Cropping can remove a table edge, desk, or empty background so the recognizer sees the useful
      region. My crop test went too far. <code>REFERENCE</code> became <code>EFERENCE</code>, and{' '}
      <code>STATUS</code> became <code>TATUS</code> because the first pixels were gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      OCR cannot reconstruct a letter outside the image. I leave a small border around the text and
      zoom in before running recognition. That one check prevents a clean-looking crop from silently
      changing a reference number, name, or status.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Blur, low contrast, and tiny text still passed this test</h2>
    <p>
      I expected all three cases to need corrections. They did not. The blurred invoice scored 96%,
      the low-contrast order scored 95%, and the tiny product line scored 94%. Each recognized line
      matched the expected text after normalization.
    </p>
    <p>
      These files were synthetic, straight, and uncluttered. A real receipt can add folds, textured
      paper, shadows, stains, mixed fonts, and compression. I would still retake a badly blurred
      photo and enlarge very small text, but I would not report a failure that this test did not show.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      The main Tesseract documentation covers resolution, borders, deskewing, binarization, and other
      preprocessing choices in its <a href="https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">image-quality guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>The matching language model restored the accents</h2>
    <p>
      The Spanish line contained <code>NÚMERO</code> and <code>DESCRIPCIÓN</code>. The English model
      returned both words without the first accent, producing two edits at 93% confidence. The
      Spanish model returned the exact line with zero edits and 96% confidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      That three-point confidence gain mattered less than the corrected characters. Choose the main
      language in the image, then check names and borrowed words that may follow another language.
      A language model can guide recognition, but it cannot guarantee every accent or proper noun.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>My browser OCR checklist</h2>
    <ol class="editorial-checklist">
      <li><strong>Keep the whole text line.</strong> Leave a small border so cropping does not remove character edges.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove glare at the source.</strong> Move the page, light, or camera before trying a filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Use enough pixels.</strong> Move closer or upscale a small image before recognition.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose the main language.</strong> Match the language file to the document when accents or scripts differ.</li>
      <li><strong>Check important fields.</strong> Compare names, dates, totals, decimal points, codes, and account numbers with the image.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep the original.</strong> OCR text is a working copy, not proof that the source said exactly the same thing.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>How the Access Free Tools OCR page handles the work</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/">Image to Text OCR Tool</a> loads Tesseract.js in
      the browser after you choose a supported image. Access Free Tools serves the worker, WebAssembly
      core, and language files from its own model directory. The tool does not upload the selected
      image to the Access Free Tools server for recognition.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browser-side processing does not mean the page makes no network requests. The support files
      still need to load, and the production site can record an anonymous tool action without storing
      the selected image or extracted text. My <a href="/blog/browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy/">browser AI privacy article</a>{' '}
      explains that distinction in more detail.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>What this experiment cannot prove</h2>
    <p>
      Six synthetic images do not form a general OCR benchmark. Another camera, font, language,
      compression level, browser, device, or Tesseract version can change the result. Tesseract's
      confidence is useful diagnostic evidence, not a promise that every character is correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the <a href="/blog/how-to-use-image-to-text-ocr-tool/">Image to Text OCR guide</a> for the
      full tool workflow. Keep sensitive documents out of any browser tool until its privacy terms
      fit your risk, and verify high-stakes text against the original document.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialArticleLayout>

<style>
  .ocr-results-table {
    min-width: 0;
    table-layout: fixed;
  }

  .ocr-results-table th:first-child {
    width: 50%;
  }

  .ocr-results-table th:nth-child(2),
  .ocr-results-table th:nth-child(3) {
    width: 25%;
  }

  .ocr-results-table th,
  .ocr-results-table td {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .ocr-results-table th,
    .ocr-results-table td {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      padding: 10px 8px;
    }
  }
</style>
